<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Foot binding</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Footbinding, Chánzú, Lotus feet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Foot binding was the Chinese custom of breaking and tightly binding the feet of young girls to change their shape and size, a painful practice that limited women's mobility and left lifelong disabilities. It may have begun among court dancers in the 10th century and spread among the elite under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, the earliest archaeological evidence coming from tombs of 1243 and 1274. It later spread to lower classes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> bans of 1636 to 1664 failed. In late imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> bound feet were a status symbol and a mark of beauty; by the 19th century perhaps 40 to 50% of Chinese women had them. Reformers and missionaries campaigned against it, and it died out in the early 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/foot-binding.jpg" alt="Foot binding" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lai Afong · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Chinese_Golden_Lily_Foot,_Lai_Afong,_c1870s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Foot binding</b> became a new fashion among elite women in the Southern Song.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
